\begin{align*}
2x-5 &= 0 \\
2x &= 5 \\
x &= \frac{5}{2}
\end{align*}

So, $f(x)$ is undefined when $x=\frac{5}{2}$.

Next, let's find the values of $x$ that make $f(f(x))$ undefined. We substitute $f(x)$ into $f$:

\begin{align*}
f(f(x)) &= f\left(\frac{1}{2x-5}\right) \\
&= \frac{1}{2\left(\frac{1}{2x-5}\right)-5} \\
&= \frac{1}{\frac{2}{2x-5}-5} \\
&= \frac{1}{\frac{2-5(2x-5)}{2x-5}} \\
&= \frac{1}{\frac{2-10x+25}{2x-5}} \\
&= \frac{1}{\frac{27-10x}{2x-5}} \\
&= \frac{2x-5}{27-10x}
\end{align*}

$f(f(x))$ is undefined when the denominator $27-10x$ is equal to zero. So, we set $27-10x=0$ and solve for $x$:

\begin{align*}
27-10x &= 0 \\
-10x &= -27 \\
x &= \frac{27}{10}
\end{align*}

So, $f(f(x))$ is undefined when $x=\frac{27}{10}$.

Therefore, the largest $x$ which is not in the domain of $g(x)=f(f(x))$ is $\boxed{\frac{27}{10}}$.

